fact WorkItem(id: "WI-094", description: some(value: "World naming predicate for webots (and other simulator) bindings. Tightens the string-typed coupling between controller code and world-file device names by modeling them as anthill facts. Three fact shapes: (1) RobotKind(name, devices: List[NamedDevice]) — devices baked into a robot proto, e.g. Mavic2Pro's gps/gyro/inertial-unit/4-propellers. (2) WorldRobot(world, name, kind, extra_devices: List[NamedDevice]) — robot instance in a specific world, with per-instance additions like a radio Emitter or Receiver. (3) NamedDevice(name, sort) — a (name, anthill-sort) pair. Plus a derived predicate has_device(world, robot, device, sort) :- WorldRobot(...), RobotKind(...), in_devices(...) that binds (world × robot × device-name) to its sort. Codegen check: every Robot.get_X(r, lit_name) call where lit_name is a String literal triggers a query has_device(?world, r.name, lit_name, X); a mismatch produces a typed warning at codegen time. Same name resolving to different sorts on different robots (radio = Emitter on leader, Receiver on followers) is naturally captured per-robot. Driver: lf1 example. First implementation: webots/robot_kinds.anthill listing Mavic2Pro's standard devices; per-world file under examples/webots-modelling/lf1/ listing the lf1 world's robot instances and extras. Codegen-side check is a follow-up under the eventual anthill-cpp-gen WI; the model itself is useful even without the check (tooling, documentation, supervisor-dispatch). Generalizes beyond webots: a Gazebo or hardware target would have its own robot kinds and world-file analogues."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Open)
